      "taskHash": "0c680d5d334b573ca449da81f5656cf14bf39f938fa99abd736cad53c2a5d5e4",
      "evidence": ".booklet-work/reasoning-ratings/calls/enrichment-maths-2-part11-20261009/644e45a5a968862bfa52c6b0e194492ea691cd6a811c7fd6660d8d04766ba9aa-1792558434878/last-message.txt"
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "In a class of 30 students, there are 4 students who enjoy English, Mathematics and Science. There are 10 who enjoy English and Mathematics, 6 who enjoy Science and English and 11 who enjoy Science and Mathematics. There are 19 students who enjoy Mathematics, 13 who enjoy English and 16 who enjoy Science. One of these students is randomly selected. Find the probability that the student enjoys:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "only Mathematics",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac1{15}$\n\nSubtract the two overlaps from the Mathematics total, adding back the triple overlap.",
          "worked": "Let $E,M,S$ denote enjoying English, Mathematics and Science respectively.\n$$\\begin{align*}n(\\text{only }M)&=19-10-11+4\\\\n(\\text{only }M)&=2.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\Pr(\\text{only }M)=\\frac2{30}=\\frac1{15}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Science and English",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac15$",
          "worked": "The 6 students enjoying Science and English include those enjoying all three subjects.\n$$\\Pr(S\\cap E)=\\frac6{30}=\\frac15.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "English or Mathematics",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{11}{15}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(E\\cup M)&=\\frac{13+19-10}{30}\\\\\\Pr(E\\cup M)&=\\frac{22}{30}=\\frac{11}{15}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "at least two different subjects",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{19}{30}$\n\nCount the three pair-only regions and the triple region once.",
          "worked": "The pair-only counts are $10-4=6$, $6-4=2$ and $11-4=7$.\n$$\\begin{align*}n(\\text{at least two})&=6+2+7+4\\\\n(\\text{at least two})&=19.\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\Pr(\\text{at least two})=\\frac{19}{30}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q4-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "English given that they enjoy Science",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac38$",
          "worked": "Of the 16 students enjoying Science, 6 enjoy English.\n$$\\Pr(E\\mid S)=\\frac6{16}=\\frac38.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q4-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Science given that they don’t enjoy English",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{10}{17}$\n\nRestrict the total to students who do not enjoy English.",
          "worked": "There are $30-13=17$ students who do not enjoy English. Of these, $16-6=10$ enjoy Science.\n$$\\Pr(S\\mid E')=\\frac{10}{17}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q4-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "all three subjects given that they enjoy English.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac4{13}$",
          "worked": "Of the 13 students enjoying English, 4 enjoy all three subjects.\n$$\\Pr(E\\cap M\\cap S\\mid E)=\\frac4{13}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
